Today’s Workflow


Clean data


-&gt;


Decide which rows count


-&gt;


Decide categories


-&gt;


group_by + summarize


-&gt;


Check against a known total



A total you cannot check is a guess.

Cycle 1: One Row Per Group
fishing &lt;- read_csv(here("class", "data", "fishing.csv")) |&gt;
  clean_names()

fishing |&gt; count(lake)                        # you know this one
fishing |&gt; summarize(total = sum(values))     # NA. Why?

fishing |&gt;
  group_by(lake) |&gt;
  summarize(
    rows      = n(),
    n_missing = sum(is.na(values)),           # report what you dropped
    total     = sum(values, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(total))

A Number That Should Worry You
fishing |&gt;
  filter(lake == "Michigan", species == "Lake Whitefish", year == 2000) |&gt;
  summarize(total = sum(values, na.rm = TRUE))
#&gt; 14381
Is that the U.S. commercial lake whitefish catch from Lake Michigan in 2000?

Cycle 2: Categories You Decide
GOAL: label what kind of row each one is. WHY: summing every row counted the same fish three times.
fishing |&gt;
  mutate(recorded = if_else(is.na(values), "missing", "recorded")) |&gt;
  count(lake, recorded)

fishing &lt;- fishing |&gt;
  mutate(
    region_level = case_when(
      region %in% us_totals     ~ "US total",
      region %in% canada_totals ~ "Canada total",
      region %in% state_totals  ~ "state total",
      .default = "area"
    )
  )

fishing |&gt; count(region_level, region)   # did every region land right?

Now The Totals Are Right
fishing |&gt;
  filter(region_level == "US total") |&gt;
  group_by(lake) |&gt;
  summarize(n_missing = sum(is.na(values)),
            total = sum(values, na.rm = TRUE),
            .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  arrange(desc(total))

Michigan first: 3,072,965 thousand lb
Saint Clair last, but 845 of its 1,171 rows are missing. Would you rank it with confidence?
